WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.920
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:17.376
Unit nineteen: Academic Vocabulary for TOEFL. Today you will learn and use common academic words across all four sections.

4
00:00:17.676 --> 00:00:20.235
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.352
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.299
Analyse.

7
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:30.003
Analyse means to study something in detail.

8
00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:32.419
Analyse the data carefully.

9
00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:35.483
Your turn. Say: Analyse.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.439
Analyse.

11
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.057
Evidence.

12
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.700
Evidence means facts that support an idea.

13
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:47.109
The evidence supports the claim.

14
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:50.125
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

15
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:53.886
Evidence.

16
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.802
Significant.

17
00:00:56.817 --> 00:00:59.780
Significant means important or large enough to matter.

18
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:01.953
A significant change.

19
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.376
Your turn. Say: Significant.

20
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:09.374
Significant.

21
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:11.153
Interpret.

22
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.881
Interpret means to explain the meaning of something.

23
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:17.216
Interpret the results.

24
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:20.817
Your turn. Say: Interpret.

25
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:24.726
Interpret.

26
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:26.775
Consequence.

27
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:30.131
Consequence means a result of an action.

28
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:32.751
The consequences are serious.

29
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.339
Your turn. Say: Consequence.

30
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.445
Consequence.

31
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:42.219
Contribute.

32
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:45.553
Contribute means to give something to a result.

33
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.790
Many factors contribute.

34
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:51.430
Your turn. Say: Contribute.

35
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:55.198
Contribute.

36
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.800
Factor.

37
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.466
Factor means one reason that affects a result.

38
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.423
A major factor.

39
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:05.704
Your turn. Say: Factor.

40
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:09.428
Factor.

41
00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:11.242
Sufficient.

42
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.774
Sufficient means enough.

43
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:15.907
Sufficient evidence.

44
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:19.378
Your turn. Say: Sufficient.

45
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.234
Sufficient.

46
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:27.614
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.843
Visal, why should I learn academic vocabulary?

48
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:44.600
University texts and talks use words like analyse, factor and evidence again and again. Knowing them helps you in Reading, Listening, Writing and Speaking.

49
00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:47.287
How do I learn a word well?

50
00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:59.163
Learn its meaning, its part of speech and one natural example. Then use it in a sentence about your own life so that it stays in your memory.

51
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.774
Which mistakes are common?

52
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:15.915
Students use big words incorrectly. A simple, accurate word is better than a difficult word used wrongly, so check collocations such as significant increase and sufficient evidence.

53
00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.555
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:23.158
Grammar focus: Strategy: meaning, form, use.

55
00:03:23.458 --> 00:03:24.679
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:27.731
Researchers analyse the data carefully.

57
00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:31.324
There was sufficient evidence to support the claim.

58
00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:39.122
Learn each academic word with its part of speech and a natural example. Use only words you can use accurately.

59
00:03:39.883 --> 00:03:44.068
Be careful! Don't say: I use difficult words wrongly.

60
00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:48.718
Say: I use words I know accurately.

61
00:03:49.018 --> 00:03:50.901
Accurate, not fancy.

62
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.561
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:56.844
Learning Words Well.

64
00:03:57.144 --> 00:04:00.060
Reaksmey keeps a notebook of academic words.

65
00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:04.552
For each word she writes the meaning and the part of speech.

66
00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:08.530
She adds an example sentence about her own studies.

67
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:12.880
She checks collocations such as significant increase.

68
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:16.296
Her writing sounds clearer and more natural.

69
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.228
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:22.083 --> 00:04:24.861
Academic words appear in blank sections.

71
00:04:25.161 --> 00:04:28.663
A, no. B, all four. C, only Speaking.

72
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.664
The answer is B: all four.

73
00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:38.339
To learn a word, learn its meaning and blank.

74
00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.141
A, colour. B, price. C, part of speech.

75
00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.199
The answer is C: part of speech.

76
00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.068
A simple accurate word is better than a blank word used wrongly.

77
00:04:53.368 --> 00:04:56.835
A, difficult. B, short. C, common.

78
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.446
The answer is A: difficult.

79
00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:05.049
'Sufficient' means blank.

80
00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:08.805
A, rare. B, enough. C, too much.

81
00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:14.134
The answer is B: enough.

82
00:05:15.283 --> 00:05:16.795
What does Reaksmey keep?

83
00:05:17.095 --> 00:05:20.482
A, a map. B, a ticket. C, a notebook.

84
00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:26.509
The answer is C: a notebook.

85
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.991
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.291
How do you learn academic vocabulary?

87
00:05:43.083 --> 00:05:44.618
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:44.918 --> 00:05:57.697
I write each word with its meaning, part of speech and an example sentence about my own life. I check collocations so that I use the word naturally. I review my notebook every week.

89
00:05:57.997 --> 00:05:59.765
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:05.450
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: meaning, form, use.

91
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.703
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

92
00:06:10.003 --> 00:06:12.644
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

93
00:06:12.944 --> 00:06:15.445
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

